1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,400
Proposition forty-two restricts certain future taxes. It focuses on owning

2
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:10,140
personal property, including financial assets, and on taxes applied

3
00:00:10,140 --> 00:00:15,580
retroactively. Personal property here generally means assets other than real

4
00:00:15,580 --> 00:00:20,760
estate. California already taxes income from assets, such as investment

5
00:00:20,760 --> 00:00:26,100
gains, and some forms of personal property already have taxes. It generally

6
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:30,860
does not tax simply owning stocks. The proposal is primarily about

7
00:00:30,860 --> 00:00:34,020
restricting new taxes, not issuing a refund.

8
00:00:34,667 --> 00:00:39,147
Imagine a business owner reviewing the value of company assets. Owning

9
00:00:39,147 --> 00:00:43,987
equipment or shares is different from earning a profit. A rule against a new

10
00:00:43,987 --> 00:00:49,567
ownership tax does not, by itself, eliminate income taxes on profits or

11
00:00:49,567 --> 00:00:55,067
every tax the owner already pays. A budget analyst considers future options

12
00:00:55,067 --> 00:00:56,387
for raising revenue.

13
00:00:56,967 --> 00:01:01,107
The measure would prohibit new state taxes on ownership of financial assets

14
00:01:01,107 --> 00:01:06,667
and other personal property. It also limits certain retroactive taxes: taxes

15
00:01:06,667 --> 00:01:10,927
applied to activities or periods before the tax was adopted. For

16
00:01:10,927 --> 00:01:15,627
illustration, a tax enacted later but applied to income earned in an earlier

17
00:01:15,627 --> 00:01:20,727
period is retroactive. The measure’s detailed rules determine what is

18
00:01:20,727 --> 00:01:24,887
prohibited; this is not a claim that every tax adjustment becomes

19
00:01:24,887 --> 00:01:30,247
impossible. The analyst does not attach a specific dollar saving or loss.

20
00:01:30,247 --> 00:01:34,027
Future revenue might rise less because some tax options would be

21
00:01:34,027 --> 00:01:38,227
unavailable. The effect depends on what lawmakers or voters would otherwise

22
00:01:38,227 --> 00:01:43,167
propose. Prop forty-two could also prevent Prop forty’s wealth tax from

23
00:01:43,167 --> 00:01:48,307
taking effect if both pass and forty-two gets more yes votes. The conflict

24
00:01:48,307 --> 00:01:52,947
and its legal interpretation matter alongside the broader restrictions.

25
00:01:53,633 --> 00:01:58,473
A voter might favor predictable rules for savings, investments, and business

26
00:01:58,473 --> 00:02:04,053
assets, or object to taxes that reach backward. They may also want to stop

27
00:02:04,053 --> 00:02:08,773
the proposed wealth tax. This is a choice about limiting government’s future

28
00:02:08,773 --> 00:02:09,993
taxing authority.

29
00:02:10,567 --> 00:02:15,287
You might want future voters to retain more tax choices. Another voter could

30
00:02:15,287 --> 00:02:19,647
prefer deciding future tax proposals individually instead of restricting

31
00:02:19,647 --> 00:02:24,447
them in the constitution. They may want wealth-based revenue options during

32
00:02:24,447 --> 00:02:30,127
a funding shortfall, or support Prop forty. Keeping an option available does

33
00:02:30,127 --> 00:02:32,767
not mean every future tax must be adopted.

34
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:38,800
In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 54 percent said yes, 43

35
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:44,220
percent no, and 4 percent were unsure. These are dated survey results, not

36
00:02:44,220 --> 00:02:49,860
votes already cast. A later September Berkeley survey found 43 percent yes,

37
00:02:49,860 --> 00:02:55,480
34 percent no, and 23 percent undecided. Question wording and methods

38
00:02:55,480 --> 00:02:59,980
differ, so comparing polls does not by itself establish a trend.

39
00:03:00,567 --> 00:03:05,327
Yes imposes the new limits; no retains the state’s current options without

40
00:03:05,327 --> 00:03:10,587
itself creating a new tax. Visit our website for the fuller explanation,

41
00:03:10,587 --> 00:03:15,067
source links, and official voter guide. Compare the tradeoffs, then make

42
00:03:15,067 --> 00:03:16,787
your own informed choice.
